WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.450
BLAKE HAWKINS: Good evening.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.770
I'm Blake Hawkins.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.710
And I am a sophomore accounting
major from Omaha, Nebraska.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.510
And tonight, I have the honor
of introducing our speaker.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.670
Our speaker is Allen C. Guelzo.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:19.830
He is a Senior Research Scholar
in the Council of Humanities

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.700
and a Director of the Initiative
on Politics and Statesmanship

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.940
in the James Madison program
at Princeton University.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.050
Dr. Guelzo earned his MA
and PhD from the University

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.070
of Pennsylvania.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.980
In September 2005,
he was nominated

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.460
by President Bush to the
National Council on Humanities.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.740
His articles and
essays have appeared

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.470
in numerous publications,
including The Wall Street

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.070
Journal, the Christian Science
Monitor, The New York Times,

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.770
The Atlantic and the
Los Angeles Times.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.790
He is the author
of several books,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.810
including Abraham Lincoln--

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.420
Redeemer President,
Lincoln and Douglas--

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.400
The Debates that
Defined America,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.870
and Fateful Lightning--

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.110
A New History of the
History of the Civil War

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.129
and Reconstruction.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.596
Without further ado, Dr. Guelzo.

00:01:07.596 --> 00:01:10.512
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.210
ALLEN GUELZO: Before I begin
to talk about General Lee

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.940
this evening, I want
to say a word of thanks

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:26.110
for the opportunity to be here
at Hillsdale College, which

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.050
is such a remarkable,
remarkable institution.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.872
I wandered around a
little bit this morning.

00:01:31.872 --> 00:01:34.720
I went over to see the
chapel, and then I went over

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.490
to the student center.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.130
And there, I saw
something I had not

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.370
seen in many, many, many years.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:46.480
I saw students diligently
bent over tables

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:54.830
with cups of coffee, reading
Homer, Pope, Latin, and not

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:59.320
only students, but some
faculty, people of my vintage,

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.340
so to speak.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:10.889
And I saw male and female
students talking to each other

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:13.790
and in an innocence that
brought to mind what it must

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.400
have been like before
the tree of the knowledge

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.591
of good and evil.

00:02:17.591 --> 00:02:19.946
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.680
But it was a
wonderful and for me a

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.690
touching moment to
see that kind of thing

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:31.680
because it has become
sad to say so very rare.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.400
There's a description in
Roger Scruton, the late Roger

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:35.900
Scruton.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:37.940
And I say that with great
sorrow because he's now

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.732
only just in the past
two weeks, of course,

00:02:39.732 --> 00:02:42.050
become the late Roger Scruton.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:49.650
And he leaves quite a space,
quite a space against the sky.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.540
But in his little book on
hunting of all subjects,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.470
he had this to say.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:04.140
"Students today, their
studies in victimology

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.260
have all but deprived
them of words.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.960
And their empty slogans
and witless sneers

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.610
make me both ashamed for them
and anxious to invite them

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.400
home for some tutorials.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.700
Between bouts of pop
music and television,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.000
they are handed
jargon-ridden drivel

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.320
by outdated Parisian
gurus, impenetrable texts

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.220
of sociology, the half
articulate leavings

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.830
of the grievance trade."

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:41.330
Scruton said what young people
need is nothing so much as wit,

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:44.420
allusion, and style.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.160
They should be studying
advocacy and argument.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.610
They should be reading
poetry, criticism,

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.930
and the authors who have
said things clearly and well.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.595
Thank you, Larry Arnn.

00:04:02.595 --> 00:04:05.505
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.100
Thank you, Larry Arnn.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:11.900
Thank you, faculty of Hillsdale.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:12.950
Thank you, trustees.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.839
Thank you all who are friends
and supporters of Hillsdale

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:20.480
for making what
Scruton described

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.660
as the real goal for education
and students a real place here.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:33.530
In Ray Bradbury's dystopian
novel Fahrenheit 451,

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.290
he described a world in
which books were bunrt.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.030
They were destroyed
because, of course,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:40.730
they might contain very
dangerous things like ideas.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:48.530
One fireman who had grown
disenchanted with this

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.850
ran away and ran in
pursuit of a rumor

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:54.980
that somewhere
out in the swamps,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:59.150
there were small colonies
of scholars and academics

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:04.880
and other runaways who preserved
the books by memorizing them.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.120
And this runaway fireman
found one of these colonies

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:08.710
in the swamps.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:10.460
And he was introduced
to the people there.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.910
And they would step up and
say, hello, I'm Descartes.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.590
I'm Descartes on
first principles.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.640
Or hello, I'm Plato,
The Symposium.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.590
They had memorized
them to preserve them

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.630
and hand them down until
a better time would

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.990
come when once again these
works could be printed,

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.270
and read, and treasured.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:37.940
And I thought today was a little
bit like being in that swamp.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:43.910
Much more upscale,
but the spirit,

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.830
the spirit was the same.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:50.420
And it has been a joy to be
here and be in the midst of it.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:02.970
No one who met Robert
Edward Lee no matter

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.370
what the circumstances
of the meeting

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:09.450
ever seemed to fail to
be impressed by the man.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:13.890
His dignity, his manners,
his composure all

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:16.740
seemed to create a
peculiar sense of awe

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.020
in the minds of observers.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.020
As a cadet at West
Point in the 1820s,

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.680
his classmate and later fellow
Confederate General Joseph E.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.090
Johnston spoke of Lee
having a dignity which

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.590
was as much a part of himself
as the elegance of his person,

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.340
which evidenced a superiority
that everyone acknowledged

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.170
in his heart.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:44.250
In the 1840s, when he was posted
as the engineering officer

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.130
at Fort Hamilton
guarding the approaches

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.670
to The Narrows of New York
City, one woman visitor

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:55.290
described Lee as a most
elegant man, quite the ideal

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:58.440
of a military hero who
received me with something more

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:02.670
than military courtesy,
bending his clear, handsome eye

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.610
upon me.

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.520
10 years later, as
the superintendent

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.390
of West Point from
1852 to 1855, he

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:15.570
appeared to the young
John Schofield, who

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.300
would go on to carve out a
distinguished career of his own

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.330
in the army, as the
personification of dignity,

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:26.130
justice, and kindness and
was respected and admired

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:29.530
as the ideal of a
commanding officer.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.880
Even in the midst of
battle at Fredericksburg

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:39.120
in December of 1862, Lee
awed Charles Francis Lawley,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.210
the London Times special
correspondent in America,

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:45.960
for the serenity or,
if I may so express it,

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.140
the unconscious dignity
of General Lee's courage

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:51.640
when he is under fire.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.720
Not even our friend
Ulysses Grant

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:01.180
could escape the sense of being
upstaged by Lee at Appomattox.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:05.200
"He was a man of much dignity
with an impassible face,

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.600
Grant wrote in his
memoirs, "dressed

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.390
in a full uniform,
which was entirely new,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.780
and wearing a sword of
considerable value."

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:19.220
While Grant was "self-conscious
of my rough traveling suit--

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.030
the uniform of a private with
the straps of a lieutenant

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.980
general sewn on.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:27.880
I must have contrasted
strangely," Grant admitted,

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.310
"with a man so
handsomely dressed,

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:33.235
six feet high and
of faultless form."

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:39.880
These impressions appear so
consistent and over so many

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.710
years that it has
been easy to conclude

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:50.200
that dignity and manners and
composure simply were the man

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.820
and that there was, as Douglas
Southall Freeman insisted

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.720
at the end of his
four-volume biography of Lee,

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:01.640
no mystery at all
to Robert E. Lee,

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:05.250
or as Burton Hendrick wrote
in The Lees of Virginia

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:10.530
that Lee's character was
ruled by a great simplicity,

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:14.400
or that in the words of an even
more worshipful biographer,

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:19.890
Clifford Dowdey, Lee could rest
totally in very simple things.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:27.550
However, this picture of
straightforward, well-nigh,

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:32.010
angelic serenity sits
uneasily beside moments

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:36.660
when cracks and inconsistencies
in that fabled serenity

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:38.700
appeared.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.380
For instance, Lee
worried constantly

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:48.070
and insistently about
money despite the fact

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:50.680
that he had married into one
of the most prominent families

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.660
in the Washington, DC, orbit--

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.860
that of George Washington
Parke Custis, the step-grandson

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:00.340
of George Washington, and
his wife Mary Fitzhugh

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:04.420
Custis, who owned the palatial
estate that they called

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:09.190
Arlington perched
palatine-like on the Potomac

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.700
bluffs overlooking
the capital city.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.640
In fact, Robert E. Lee
worried about money

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:17.950
even though
according to the will

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:22.150
he filed before going off
to fight in the Mexican War,

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:26.200
he had actually inherited quite
a healthy sum from his mother

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:30.280
and Carter Lee and had
invested with enough success

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:37.100
to have acquired a
portfolio worth $38,750.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.350
Translated into
2020 dollars, that's

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:41.930
worth about $1.2 million.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:46.370
It made no difference.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:50.000
At the same moment, he assured
his West Point classmate Jack

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.640
Mackay that, "It
was as much as I

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.580
could do to make both
ends meet this year,

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.550
and I am anxiously looking for
the dividend of the Virginia

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:02.810
bank to enable me to
square off all scores."

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:05.360
When Lee left Fort
Hamilton in 1847

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:09.080
for duty in the Mexican War,
he complained to his brother

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:13.040
that, "I fear I have
not enough to take

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:17.370
me and my servant to the
Army and purchase my horses."

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:21.540
When he transferred out of
the Corps of Engineers in 1855

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:25.680
to become Lieutenant Colonel
of the 2nd cavalry in Texas,

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.170
he insisted on writing
checks on his Alexandria bank

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:36.960
from Texas for his wife at
Arlington to deposit for cash

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.610
and all the while chiding her to
be "very particular, dear Mary,

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:45.600
when you deal in money matters
and to keep a memorandum book

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.540
and set everything down."

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.810
His advice for his
youngest son in 1856

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:56.400
was to cultivate
industry and frugality,

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.690
and be prudent
before he is liberal,

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:03.085
and be just before
he is generous.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:10.490
Other kinds of cracks
opened under pressure.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:13.310
Trained as an engineer
and the director

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:16.010
of a series of demanding
coastal engineering

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:19.910
projects from controlling the
silting up of the St. Louis

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:23.600
waterfront to the construction
of Fort Carroll in Baltimore

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:27.890
Harbor, Lee was at his happiest
with a draftsman's notebook

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:34.190
in hand and impatient at
every other sort of paperwork.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:38.120
"I abhor the sight of pen
and paper," he admitted.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.010
"And to put one to
the other requires

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:45.650
as great a moral effort as for
a cat to walk on live coals."

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:48.140
This did not prevent
him from writing over

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:51.800
4,000 letters in his
lifetime, but that

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.620
was personal correspondence.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:58.110
Official papers were his bane.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:02.550
He also grew impatient
when matters spilled out

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:08.520
of the kind of perfection that
tea squares and equations can

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.080
impose.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:13.630
After the close of the
Maryland campaign in 1862,

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:18.840
he unleashed on his two chief
subordinates, James Longstreet

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:22.080
and Stonewall Jackson,
a long lecture

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.590
by letter on the
depredations committed

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:28.800
by this army, its
daily diminution,

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.280
and a loss of arms thrown
aside as too burdensome

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.970
by stragglers.

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.920
It would be impossible to
imagine Ulysses Grant writing

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.600
a letter like this to William
Tecumseh Sherman or James B.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.970
McPherson.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:50.070
But for Lee, no shortcoming
in either of these commanders

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.290
missed his perfect eye.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.270
"Roll calls are neglected.

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:56.280
And officers of
companies and regiments

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.800
are ignorant of the true
condition of their commands

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.520
and are unable to account
properly for absentees."

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:05.280
He now wanted a brigade
guard to bring up

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.430
the rear of each brigade
to keep up the ranks,

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.610
drive up all stragglers
irrespective of commands

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:13.720
and all leaving the ranks.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:17.940
"We must," he demanded,
"infuse a different spirit

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:21.210
among our officers,"
which was more or less

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:26.070
what he found a begging in
the Confederacy as a whole.

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.670
"Our people are so
little liable to control

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:32.670
that it is difficult to get
them to follow any course not

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.380
in accordance with
their inclination,"

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:39.040
he admitted to Justus Scheibert,
the Prussian military observer.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:42.750
And when Scheibert expressed
frankly my admiration

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:48.090
for the lion-like bravery
of his men, Lee disagreed.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:52.230
"Give me also Prussian
discipline and Prussian forms.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:56.350
And you would see quite
different results."

00:14:56.350 --> 00:15:03.030
That perfectionism was linked
to a more easily agitated temper

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.860
than easily met the eye.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:09.760
Walter Taylor, who as Lee's
adjutant during the Civil War

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.030
had to sort and docket
the incoming mail,

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:17.870
discovered that Lee had lost
nothing of his engineer's

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:21.170
distaste for office
work, especially

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:24.170
if it was "heavy with
matters of detail coming

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:26.420
from the personal
commands of our army

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:29.600
which demanded the personal
consideration and decision

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:31.940
of the commander of the Army."

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:38.000
If Taylor brought him some
case of a vexatious character,

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:42.230
Lee's irritation would
flare up gradually,

00:15:42.230 --> 00:15:47.120
beginning with a little nervous
twist or jerk of the neck

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:51.980
and head and eventually erupting
into a full-fledged argument.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.760
"He is so unreasonable
and provoking,"

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:59.270
Taylor raged to his
fiancee in a letter.

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:02.630
"I never worked so hard to
please anyone and with so

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.970
little effect as General Lee."

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:10.800
Charles Marshall, who handled
Lee's outgoing correspondence,

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:15.480
had an only slightly easier
task because Lee either

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:19.770
dictated first drafts or allowed
Marshall to draw up documents

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:20.640
himself.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.940
But then Lee "weighed
every sentence

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:28.110
I wrote, frequently making
minute verbal alterations

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.570
and questioned me closely
as to the evidence

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:32.550
on which I based
all statements which

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:35.550
he did not know to be correct."

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:39.210
He did not allow any friend
of soldiers condemned

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:43.440
by court martial to reach
his tent for personal appeal,

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.200
remembered Charles
Venable, another member

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.820
of his civil war staff.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:51.270
And when such a disappointed
man elbowed their way

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:54.210
into his presence,
Lee would bark

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:56.910
not only at the
petitioner, but at Venable

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.310
for not preventing
the annoyance.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:02.580
"Why did you permit that
man to come to my tent

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:05.730
and make me show my temper?"

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.849
One of Stonewall
Jackson's staff officers

00:17:08.849 --> 00:17:14.260
asked an innocent question
while making a report to Lee.

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:16.710
And Lee's response
at this impertinence

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:20.940
was so frosty that the
staffer remarked, "I never

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:24.390
felt so small in my life."

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:28.670
Even Lee's son, George
Washington Custis Lee,

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:31.860
who was attached to Confederate
President Jefferson Davis's

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:36.240
personal staff, had to endure
his father's flare-ups,

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:39.750
reading letters from
his father complaining

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:42.060
that he was so tired
of the pettiness

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:44.400
of the Confederate
Congress that he wished

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:46.380
they "would pass
a law relieving me

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:49.980
from all duty and legislating
someone in my place

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.050
better able to do it.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:55.380
I fear you will think
I am in a bad humor.

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:56.805
And I fear I am."

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:04.940
But the deepest mark of
the depths of Lee's temper

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:09.230
occurred in the spring of 1859.

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:12.530
When his father-in-law
died in 1857,

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:16.730
Lee was named executor of
the Custis estate, which

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:22.190
to his surprise required him
to superintend the emancipation

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:27.650
within five years of all the
Custis slaves at Arlington

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:31.120
and two other
Custis-owned properties.

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:34.420
Lee could not hurry to
execute this emancipation

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:39.190
because the will also
mandated that four $10,000

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:43.700
legacies be paid to
each of Lee's daughters.

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:47.150
Since the properties had been
run haphazardly for years

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:50.090
by old Custis, Lee
would need to turn them

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:53.960
into engines of efficiency
to pay the required legacies.

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:59.200
And that he did with an
engineer's sense of precision.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:03.330
But the Custis slaves did
not share his interest

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.450
in making Arlington and the
other places profitable.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:09.810
They believed that the
Custis will had in fact

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.970
emancipated them at once.

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:17.280
And in 1858, three of
them, two men and a woman,

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:21.720
acted on that belief and fled
to Westminster, Maryland,

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:25.050
where they were eventually
apprehended and returned

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.310
to Arlington.

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:31.940
His fabled self-control
teetering unsteadily,

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:36.470
Lee demanded of the
three why they ran away.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:43.590
Because, they replied, frankly,
we considered ourselves free.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:48.450
That to Robert E. Lee was not
merely a legal misapprehension.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:51.690
It was a threat to his own
integrity as the Custis will's

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:55.940
executor and the future
of his daughters.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.330
He then told us
he would "teach us

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:02.260
a lesson we would never
forget," and ordered

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:07.000
the three stripped to the waist,
and directed the Arlington

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:12.970
overseer, John McQuinn, to give
the men 50 lashes each and 20

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.230
to the woman.

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:22.230
McQuinn balked and had
sufficient humanity to decline.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:24.680
So Lee turned to Richard
Williams, the Arlington

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:27.530
Constable who had brought
the fugitives back,

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.770
and had him perform
the whipping while Lee

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:35.400
demanded he lay it on well.

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:39.030
By one account, Lee took
the whip in his own hands

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:40.170
and flayed them himself.

00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:49.270
Afterwards, he was so
appalled at his own rage

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:52.480
that he could not admit the
full extent of what he had done,

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:55.690
even to his son Custis,
who read about the incident

00:20:55.690 --> 00:20:58.420
in the newspapers.

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:00.220
"The New-York Tribune
has attacked me

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:03.670
for my treatment of your
grandfather's slaves,"

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:06.050
he conceded to Custis.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:08.860
"But I shall not reply.

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:12.580
Your grandfather," he
added, "has left me

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:13.625
an unpleasant legacy."

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:23.640
Robert E. Lee's anxieties, his
impatience, and his dark temper

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:27.840
have, since the publication
of Thomas Connolly's

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:31.500
melodramatic recasting of
Lee's historical reputation

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.700
in The Marble Man--

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:39.060
Robert E. Lee and His Image
in American Society in 1977,

00:21:39.060 --> 00:21:42.480
been seized upon as a
triumphant contradiction

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:46.470
of the legendary Lee who so
impressed his contemporaries

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:48.840
civilian and military.

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:53.550
To Alan Nolan in 1991, Lee
was just a typical southern

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.790
partisan with all the
southern aristocrats' feeling

00:21:56.790 --> 00:21:59.880
for the inferiority
of northern people.

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:03.340
To Michael Fellman in
2000, Lee was really

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:08.890
an exemplar of the dark side
of the white supremacist road.

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:11.980
To Roy Blount, probably
the most unlikely

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:16.300
of all Lee biographers,
in 2003, Lee

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:22.470
was mentally and
pathologically disturbed.

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:30.270
But casting Lee in contradiction
as either saint or as sinner,

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:34.290
as either simple
or pathological,

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:40.140
is in the end less profitable
than seeing his anxieties

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:44.250
as a counterpoint to his
dignity, his impatience

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:47.490
and his temper as a
match to his composure.

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:56.000
To begin to understand the
mystery of Robert E. Lee

00:22:56.000 --> 00:23:00.710
is to begin with three
large-scale factors lodged deep

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:03.490
in the man's personality.

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:06.450
One of these was the
desire for perfection,

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.730
then the desire
for independence,

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:12.930
and then the desire
for security.

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:17.490
All three were rooted in
the early trauma inflicted

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:21.450
by one of the more remarkably
dysfunctional families

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.970
of the early Republic.

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:27.000
Up through the revolution,
the Lees of Virginia

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:31.140
had been one of the first of
American families presiding

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:35.290
over large properties on the
northern neck of Virginia.

00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:37.530
And in the case of
the four Lee brothers

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:40.740
who straddled the revolutionary
years, Richard Henry

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:45.570
Lee, Francis Lightfoot Lee,
Arthur and William Lee,

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:48.990
they were that band
of brothers, according

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:52.830
to John Adams, intrepid
and unchangeable

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:55.770
who stood in the gap in
defense of their country

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:57.990
from the first glimmering
of the revolution

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:02.510
through all its rising
light to its perfect day.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:04.860
Richard Henry Lee and
Francis Lightfoot Lee

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.050
both served in the
Continental Congress

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.120
at the outbreak
of the revolution.

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:11.070
And they are the
only brothers who

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:14.250
appear as signers of the
Declaration of Independence.

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:18.060
Arthur Lee would turn into
an outstanding diplomat

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:19.500
in Spain and France.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.620
And William Lee would
represent American interests

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:26.580
in the German
states and Austria.

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:32.790
But something in the succeeding
generation of Lees snapped.

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:35.430
And nowhere was the snap
louder than in the gentleman

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:40.440
whose picture is on the screen
behind me, that of Henry Lee

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:44.050
the third, Robert's father.

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:48.840
Henry Lee was a graduate
of Princeton, my employer,

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:53.730
in the same class of
1771 as James Madison.

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:56.820
Henry Lee served George
Washington so effectively

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:01.630
as a cavalry leader that he won
the nickname Light Horse Harry.

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:03.620
And in the glow
of the revolution,

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:07.760
Light Horse Harry captured not
only the hand of his cousin

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:13.420
Matilda and her family's
estate Stratford Hall,

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:16.210
but he also captured the
governorship of Virginia.

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:20.440
Nothing seemed beyond his grasp.

00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:25.770
After that, however, Light
Horse Harry disintegrated.

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.740
His ardent federalism
antagonized

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:31.170
Virginia's Jeffersonians.

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:36.030
His harebrained investment
schemes bankrupted him.

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:38.640
After Matilda's
death, he attempted

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:41.670
to recoup his fortune
by marrying a Virginia

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:45.690
Carter, Anne Hill Carter.

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:50.520
But he burned
through her cash too

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:52.980
or at least the
limited amount of cash

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:57.290
her father had prudently put
at the newlyweds' disposal.

00:25:57.290 --> 00:26:00.360
If Henry Lee were
alive today, he'd

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:03.290
invest in ski resorts
in Bangladesh.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:11.310
After he spent a stint
in debtor's prison,

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:14.310
the sadder but wiser
Anne Carter Lee

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:16.860
demanded that her family,
which now numbered

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:20.250
three sons, including
Robert, and two daughters,

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:25.510
move to more manageable
quarters in Alexandria.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:28.530
But Light Horse Harry could
not shake his penchant

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:30.510
for disaster.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:33.060
After being beaten within
an inch of his life

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:37.500
in an Anti-Federalist
riot in Baltimore in 1812,

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:40.740
Light Horse Harry decamped
the following year

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:44.640
for the safety of
the West Indies.

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:49.850
Robert never saw
his father again.

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:54.000
He was six years old.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:27:01.170
Light Horse Harry only returned
to America fatally ill in 1818,

00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:03.780
barely making
landfall in Georgia

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:09.770
before dying and leaving Robert
to grow up in literal as well

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:14.360
as figurative terms fatherless.

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:16.670
"There is," wrote
the literary critic

00:27:16.670 --> 00:27:22.640
Leon Edel, "no hurt
among all the human

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:27.410
hurts deeper and less
understandable than the loss

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:32.770
of a parent when one is
not yet an adolescent."

00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:35.180
And Robert E. Lee offers
us a textbook case

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:36.900
in the truth of that saying.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:41.630
Until he achieved fame
in his own right in 1862,

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:44.150
people invariably
referred to Robert

00:27:44.150 --> 00:27:48.680
as the son of the famed
Light Horse Harry,

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:52.340
but he never did, except
in his application letter

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:54.620
to West Point.

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:57.770
He did not visit
his father's grave

00:27:57.770 --> 00:28:02.660
until the winter of 1861 to 62
even though his first posting

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:05.240
out of West Point
to Cockspur Island

00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:07.990
was only a few miles away.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:12.600
He was instead his mother's
son, becoming the de facto head

00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:14.430
of household in Alexandria.

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:18.300
It was Robert who carried
the keys, attended

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:21.630
to the marketing, managed
all the outdoor business,

00:28:21.630 --> 00:28:24.840
and took care of
his mother's horses.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:28.220
When his mother was
ill, it was Robert

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:32.150
who ordered up the
family's shabby brougham

00:28:32.150 --> 00:28:36.650
and then would be seen carrying
her in his arms to the carriage

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:39.350
and arranging her cushions
with the gentleness

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:42.350
of an experienced nurse.

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:46.880
One of the flock of Lee
cousins in Alexandria

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:50.480
remembered that Robert became
legendary for his devotion

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:52.460
to his mother and
the great help he

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:56.750
was to her in all her
business on household matters.

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:59.300
Years later, he would
simply describe himself

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:05.690
as my mother's outdoor agent
and confidential messenger.

00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:09.980
He would in other
words fulfill the role

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:12.440
his father had abandoned.

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:15.230
He would sacrifice
himself in order

00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:18.530
to perfect the
imperfections Light Horse

00:29:18.530 --> 00:29:23.480
Harry had visited on the Lees.

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:28.160
This pursuit of
redemptive perfection

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:32.210
lies behind much of
the marble model that

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:34.880
met so many people's eyes.

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:39.560
And it was Lee's determination
to be not Light Horse Harry

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:42.800
when everyone expected
him to be a reprise of his

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:48.530
ne'er-do-well father which fired
his impatience and eventually

00:29:48.530 --> 00:29:51.230
his ferocious outburst
of temper at his own

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:54.650
and others' imperfections.

00:29:54.650 --> 00:29:58.580
That did not mean that Robert
would enjoy the shackles

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:01.310
and demands of perfection.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:03.740
And it came as a shock
to Anne Carter Lee

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:07.400
when, in 1824, Robert
announced his desire

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:12.770
to leave her home in Alexandria
and attend West Point.

00:30:12.770 --> 00:30:15.110
"How can I live without Robert?"

00:30:15.110 --> 00:30:15.950
she wailed.

00:30:15.950 --> 00:30:19.610
"He is both son and
daughter to me."

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:22.160
She would have
been more disturbed

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:23.950
if she could have
sensed how much Robert,

00:30:23.950 --> 00:30:27.020
for all of his uncomplaining
self-sacrifice,

00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:31.910
longed to be free and
unencumbered of his mother

00:30:31.910 --> 00:30:34.940
as much as his father.

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:40.220
"I thought," he wrote, "and
intended always to be one

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:43.150
and alone in the world.

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:47.320
I am fond of
independence," Lee wrote.

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.660
And that, as he
explained in 1851,

00:30:49.660 --> 00:30:53.140
was linked to his
perfectionist impulse.

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:57.520
"It is that feeling that
prompts me to come up strictly

00:30:57.520 --> 00:31:00.340
to the requirements of
law and regulations.

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:04.360
I wish neither to seek or
receive indulgence from anyone.

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:09.070
I wish to feel under
obligation to no one."

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:12.340
The problem with
longing for independence

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:17.300
is that it does not
guarantee security.

00:31:17.300 --> 00:31:22.550
And security was precisely
the damaging subtraction

00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:26.270
Light Horse Harry
made in Robert's life.

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:30.030
So as much as Robert Lee
longed to be his own man,

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:34.490
he was also aware that the
independent man could very well

00:31:34.490 --> 00:31:39.590
be the impoverished,
lonely, neglected man.

00:31:39.590 --> 00:31:42.170
And he did not
want that, either.

00:31:42.170 --> 00:31:45.500
One of the major attractions
of a career in the US Army

00:31:45.500 --> 00:31:50.210
was its guarantee of
lifetime employment security.

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:53.840
For the tiny cadre of officers
who commanded the pre-Civil War

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:57.270
army, there was no
retirement system.

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:01.640
Once in, many stayed
in and at paid rank

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:04.310
until their last breath.

00:32:04.310 --> 00:32:07.100
The army was not generous.

00:32:07.100 --> 00:32:09.600
And promotion was
agonizingly slow.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.080
But it was one of
the few professions

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:16.850
in the pre-Civil War
republic which was secure.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:20.180
"All my schemes of
happiness," he wrote,

00:32:20.180 --> 00:32:23.480
"depend on retiring to some
quiet corner among the hills

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:26.750
of Virginia, where I can
indulge my natural propensities

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:29.430
without interruption."

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:32.340
But when his older
brother Carter

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:34.770
tried to persuade
him to leave the army

00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:40.590
and do just that with the
17,000 acres in hardy Patrick

00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:42.330
and Shenandoah
counties that they

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:47.640
had inherited from their
father, Robert hesitated.

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:50.130
"Perhaps the charms
of your establishment

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:56.740
may win me from Uncle Sam,"
he replied to Carter in 1832.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:33:00.130
But in the end, he decided not.

00:33:00.130 --> 00:33:03.490
The army was his place
"for better, for worse,

00:33:03.490 --> 00:33:05.790
as we say in matrimony."

00:33:05.790 --> 00:33:09.520
And he would have to
relinquish such prospects

00:33:09.520 --> 00:33:15.660
as Western Virginia offered
to "bachelors like yourself."

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.960
Matrimony was yet
another path to security.

00:33:18.960 --> 00:33:25.020
For in 1831, Lee married
Mary Anna Randolph Custis,

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:29.550
the sole surviving child
of the Arlington Custises,

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:33.210
and thereby won himself a
permanent home at Arlington

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:36.270
for the next 30 years.

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:38.850
The difficulty was that
the security represented

00:33:38.850 --> 00:33:42.030
by the Custis marriage
and Arlington did not

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:47.080
sit very easily beside Lee's
yearning for independence.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:51.060
His Custis in-laws made
no secret of their desire

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:54.840
to withdraw Robert from
his present professions

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:59.070
and yield to him the
management of affairs.

00:33:59.070 --> 00:34:03.930
But Lee had no desire to
abandon the army merely

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:08.679
to become the glorified
overseer of the Custises.

00:34:08.679 --> 00:34:12.360
Despite his very real affection
for his mother-in-law,

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:20.940
Lee found his father-in-law
an amiable but annoying dunce.

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:25.469
"The major," the elderly
Custis's honorary militia

00:34:25.469 --> 00:34:32.350
title, "the major is busy
farming," Lee wrote to Carter.

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:35.230
Busy was sardonic.

00:34:35.230 --> 00:34:39.670
"His cornfield is not
yet enclosed or plowed,

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:43.570
but he is rushing
on all he knows."

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:46.630
The old man had tried his
hand at writing a Sir Walter

00:34:46.630 --> 00:34:49.810
Scott-ish play about 13th
century Scotland, which

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:55.850
he called Montgomerie or
The Orphan of a Wreck.

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:59.050
It ran for four nights.

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:04.510
And so as Lee chuckled,
Montgomerie failed.

00:35:04.510 --> 00:35:09.430
Worse still, one of Custis's six
amateurish history paintings,

00:35:09.430 --> 00:35:14.440
the Battle of Trenton, has
been exhibited in the capital

00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:17.800
and met with more or
less the same response.

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:20.890
It attracted some
severe animant versions

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:26.020
from the critics, which he says
were leveled at his politics.

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:29.170
Lee knew better.

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:32.830
Seven months into
the Civil War, when

00:35:32.830 --> 00:35:36.370
Arlington had been lost
to union occupation,

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:39.850
Lee shrugged off
the estate's loss

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:42.700
and turned his attention
to the possibility

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:47.630
of acquiring another
property, nothing less

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:52.300
than his one-time boyhood
home, Stratford Hall.

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:54.940
"Stratford," he wrote,
"is endeared to me

00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:56.440
by many recollections.

00:35:56.440 --> 00:35:59.230
And it has always been a
great desire of my life

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:02.070
to be able to purchase it."

00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:05.580
He might have found security
in attaching himself

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:09.480
to the Custises, but he
yearned for the independence

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:11.670
of being a Lee.

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:16.370
And nothing symbolized
that more than Stratford.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:19.950
And perhaps it
was no coincidence

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:23.460
that two months later,
Robert E. Lee finally

00:36:23.460 --> 00:36:26.040
paid his father's
grave on Cumberland

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:28.460
Island a long delayed visit.

00:36:38.060 --> 00:36:42.740
Robert E. Lee was not
a profound thinker.

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:46.370
His compulsive letter writing
betrays little evidence

00:36:46.370 --> 00:36:50.030
of reading beyond the
demands of his profession.

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:53.480
But he was a clear thinker.

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:56.810
And much of that thinking
oscillated among the poles

00:36:56.810 --> 00:37:00.980
he had set up for himself
of perfection, independence,

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:02.780
and security.

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:05.930
And that was particularly
true in his role

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:09.050
as the Confederacy's most
successful and influential

00:37:09.050 --> 00:37:10.520
soldier.

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:13.940
Lee was incessant in his
demands on the Confederacy's

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:17.450
civilian leadership for
supplies, conscripts,

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:20.210
and recruits, and
for the perfection

00:37:20.210 --> 00:37:23.090
on the part of his officers.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:27.920
Time and again, Lee complained,
"Beaten federal armies will get

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:32.830
away because I cannot have
my orders carried out."

00:37:32.830 --> 00:37:36.790
When one hapless brigadier had
to report the federal escape

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:41.210
after Chancellorsville,
Lee's fury was volcanic.

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:46.450
"General Pender, that is
what you young men always do.

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:50.890
You allow those
people to get away."

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:54.930
But beyond the demand
for perfection,

00:37:54.930 --> 00:38:00.090
Lee also saw more clearly than
any other Confederate leader

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:04.830
that the South could not
survive a long, drawn-out bout

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:06.510
with the North.

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:09.420
Independence and
security both dictated

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:14.820
that Southern armies must cross
Maryland into Pennsylvania

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:19.160
and there convince
Northerners, either by battle

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:22.970
or by simple occupation,
to agree to peace

00:38:22.970 --> 00:38:25.270
and Southern independence.

00:38:25.270 --> 00:38:30.500
He would attempt this
twice in 1862 and 1863.

00:38:30.500 --> 00:38:33.820
And he was ready for a
third attempt in 1864

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:39.260
when Grant's Overland Campaign
struck that option away.

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:44.780
This strategy, which some have
characterized as audacity,

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:48.180
was for Lee merely logic.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:50.940
Lee's modern critics
have railed against what

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:54.120
they decry as Lee's
obsession with Virginia

00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:56.220
at the expense of the
rest of the Confederacy.

00:38:56.220 --> 00:38:59.590
But Lee understood
that the Confederacy

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:04.240
could lose Tennessee,
Mississippi, Alabama, even

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:07.780
Georgia, and still win.

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:11.860
Losing Virginia meant
losing nearly 20%

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:14.370
of the Confederacy's
white population,

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:19.510
17% of its banking capital, a
third of its industrial goods,

00:39:19.510 --> 00:39:22.860
and 40% of its wheat.

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:25.800
Richmond alone held the
Confederacy's depots

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:27.730
and arsenals.

00:39:27.730 --> 00:39:30.620
And through Richmond, Lee's
army had its chief means

00:39:30.620 --> 00:39:33.950
of access to sources of
supply further south.

00:39:33.950 --> 00:39:38.240
He dreaded nothing
more than being forced

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:41.500
into a siege of Richmond.

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:45.330
"He frequently spoke,"
recalled Charles Marshall,

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:47.640
"to the effect that if
the siege of Richmond

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:51.990
were once undertaken by an army
too strong to be beaten off,

00:39:51.990 --> 00:39:55.200
the fall of the place
would be inevitable.

00:39:55.200 --> 00:40:00.420
And with it would go Virginia
and the Confederacy," which

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:04.320
in 1865 is exactly
what happened.

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:07.680
Lee had foreseen it
all and understood it

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:13.880
as a failure to meet the
standard of perfect commitment.

00:40:13.880 --> 00:40:16.180
"It is so difficult,"
he admitted to his wife,

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:20.650
"to get our people unaccustomed
to the necessities of war

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:24.670
to comprehend the measures
required for the occasion.

00:40:24.670 --> 00:40:29.950
Instead, they failed the
standard of perfect commitment

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:33.850
that independence and
security required."

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:38.170
Lee told William
Mahone at Appomattox

00:40:38.170 --> 00:40:40.840
that he had advised the
Confederate authorities

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:44.350
at the start that the contest
on which we had entered

00:40:44.350 --> 00:40:46.220
could not be overestimated.

00:40:46.220 --> 00:40:48.010
And our chance to
win was to be found

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:50.650
by throwing the whole
military or fighting

00:40:50.650 --> 00:40:54.730
power of the Confederacy
into the struggle.

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:59.430
And this, he said,
manifestly had not been done.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:09.400
Yet, this was not the final
chapter for Robert E. Lee.

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:14.710
In the summer of 1865, he
was made the unlikely offer

00:41:14.710 --> 00:41:20.660
of the Presidency of Washington
College in far away Lexington,

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:22.240
Virginia.

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:25.180
Almost as unlikely, he accepted.

00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:27.430
And in the final five
years of his life,

00:41:27.430 --> 00:41:31.930
he managed to achieve the
resolution of the impulses

00:41:31.930 --> 00:41:35.560
to perfection, independence,
and security that he

00:41:35.560 --> 00:41:38.800
had spent a lifetime seeking.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:42.280
The trustees of the
college were congratulated

00:41:42.280 --> 00:41:45.860
on acquiring a figurehead.

00:41:45.860 --> 00:41:49.630
But Lee made them
the figureheads.

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:52.840
And he turned the
nearly bankrupt college

00:41:52.840 --> 00:41:55.690
into a southern
educational powerhouse,

00:41:55.690 --> 00:41:59.330
outstripping even the
University of Virginia.

00:41:59.330 --> 00:42:02.110
When he assumed the
presidency in 1865,

00:42:02.110 --> 00:42:07.300
Washington College had only
17 students on the rolls.

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:12.820
After one year, Lee
had built that to 146.

00:42:12.820 --> 00:42:18.600
By 1868, enrollment
stood at 411.

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:20.760
He also took in hand
the reconstruction

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:25.650
of the curriculum, sidelining
the traditional studies

00:42:25.650 --> 00:42:29.400
of Greek and Latin, one thing
for which I have not yet

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:35.520
forgiven him, in favor
of recreating Washington

00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:40.410
College as a commercial
school to give instruction

00:42:40.410 --> 00:42:45.900
in bookkeeping and the forms
and of details of business.

00:42:45.900 --> 00:42:49.140
"No wonder," he would
remark looking back that,

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:51.360
"the great mistake
of my life was

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:55.590
taking a military education."

00:42:55.590 --> 00:42:59.340
A small, remote college
would become instead

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:03.930
the place where he
would find independence

00:43:03.930 --> 00:43:08.700
to run matters as he saw
fit, security for himself

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:12.112
and his family, and
perfection in what he

00:43:12.112 --> 00:43:13.320
could demand of his students.

00:43:16.190 --> 00:43:19.310
Perhaps, in
retrospect, we can say

00:43:19.310 --> 00:43:22.790
that Robert Lee should
have shrugged off

00:43:22.790 --> 00:43:26.460
the shadow of Light Horse Harry.

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:29.670
Perhaps, he should
have left the army

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:34.080
and built a real estate
empire in Western Virginia.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:38.670
Perhaps, he should have become
the handsome but aloof family

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:44.560
overseer of Arlington and let
the Civil War wash past him,

00:43:44.560 --> 00:43:47.090
but he did not.

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:51.830
And the forces that made
him what he was in the past

00:43:51.830 --> 00:43:55.810
governed the extraordinary
skill with which he managed

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:59.210
Confederate military affairs.

00:43:59.210 --> 00:44:05.020
Those forces did not,
however, make him a happy man.

00:44:05.020 --> 00:44:10.650
That only came at
the end in Lexington,

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:15.070
but it was a perfect end.

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:16.256
Thank you very much.

00:44:16.256 --> 00:44:19.658
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:28.892 --> 00:44:32.190
AUDIENCE: Thank you, Dr. Guelzo.

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:35.822
We now have time for Q&amp;A. Please
make your way to the microphone

00:44:35.822 --> 00:44:36.780
if you have a question.

00:44:42.670 --> 00:44:44.640
AUDIENCE: Thank you, doctor.

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:49.000
I'd like you to comment on a man
who is often quoted as saying,

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:50.850
do your duty in all things.

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:52.260
You can never do more.

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:53.408
You should never do less.

00:44:53.408 --> 00:44:55.450
And then he violated his
oath to the Constitution

00:44:55.450 --> 00:44:56.730
of the United States.

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:03.630
ALLEN GUELZO: When I look at
the figure of Robert E. Lee,

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:05.280
I look at it--

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:07.230
and this will surprise nobody--

00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:10.350
as a Yankee from Yankee land.

00:45:10.350 --> 00:45:14.520
So I am an unlikely Lee
biographer to say the least.

00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:19.260
And that never comes more to
a point than on the question

00:45:19.260 --> 00:45:23.040
that I'm probably most
frequently asked this way.

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:27.210
Did Robert E. Lee
commit treason?

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:29.720
And I think there's
only one answer to that.

00:45:29.720 --> 00:45:31.130
And it's inescapable.

00:45:31.130 --> 00:45:33.200
The answer is yes.

00:45:33.200 --> 00:45:35.680
Yes, he committed treason.

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:40.270
He had served in the United
States Army for 30-some years.

00:45:40.270 --> 00:45:41.580
He had sworn the oath.

00:45:41.580 --> 00:45:45.945
He had fought under the
stars and stripes in Mexico.

00:45:45.945 --> 00:45:50.930
And he consciously turned
against all of that.

00:45:50.930 --> 00:45:54.560
And I don't have another
way of speaking of Lee

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:57.540
except to use that word.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:00.830
It is the Constitution's
definition.

00:46:00.830 --> 00:46:06.320
Constitution defines treason as
making war against the United

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:11.550
States and giving aid and
comfort to its enemies.

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:14.710
I don't have some mystical way
of being able to get around

00:46:14.710 --> 00:46:18.970
that and seeing Robert E. Lee
as not capturing precisely

00:46:18.970 --> 00:46:22.870
that reprehensible behavior.

00:46:22.870 --> 00:46:28.413
I understand that there
were many contingencies.

00:46:28.413 --> 00:46:30.080
And there were many
qualifications which

00:46:30.080 --> 00:46:34.400
went into his decision in 1861.

00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:38.000
Yet, at the same time,
I have in past time

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:42.500
sworn an oath to preserve,
protect, and defend

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:43.340
the Constitution.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:45.980
My father swore that oath.

00:46:45.980 --> 00:46:47.480
My son has sworn that oath.

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:53.830
And whether it's
Robert E. Lee or not,

00:46:53.830 --> 00:46:56.920
when you raise your
hand against that flag

00:46:56.920 --> 00:47:01.120
and that Constitution,
you are my enemy.

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:04.260
And I will regard you as such.

00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:05.790
And I do not
hesitate to say that

00:47:05.790 --> 00:47:10.160
if by some odd
transposition of time

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:13.220
I should find myself with
Robert E. Lee in my gun sights,

00:47:13.220 --> 00:47:17.410
I would not hesitate
to pull the trigger.

00:47:17.410 --> 00:47:21.780
I don't say that with
pride or pleasure,

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:24.330
but I say it as a
reality recognizing

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:26.880
the seriousness of what he did.

00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:31.390
He was at the close of the
war indicted for treason.

00:47:31.390 --> 00:47:32.800
It never came to trial.

00:47:32.800 --> 00:47:34.840
There were a number
of legal complications

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:38.300
which got in the way
of anything like that.

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:40.480
But if we are to
look at Robert E. Lee

00:47:40.480 --> 00:47:44.510
and see him as we
should see him,

00:47:44.510 --> 00:47:47.630
as a citizen who has obligations
under the Constitution

00:47:47.630 --> 00:47:51.380
of the United States,
then there really

00:47:51.380 --> 00:47:55.730
is no other word that
we can use and no way

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:58.580
to escape from that word.

00:47:58.580 --> 00:48:01.640
Robert E. Lee committed treason.

00:48:01.640 --> 00:48:03.800
I don't have a better
way of putting it.

00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:05.606
I hope that answers
the question.

00:48:05.606 --> 00:48:08.402
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:11.210 --> 00:48:14.480
AUDIENCE: We have time
for one more question.

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:18.530
AUDIENCE: There are historians
in the medical military history

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:22.652
that speak to Robert E.
Lee's state of health,

00:48:22.652 --> 00:48:24.860
especially his problems with
congestive heart failure

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:26.652
and how they may have
affected his judgment

00:48:26.652 --> 00:48:28.940
and his ability to
command during Gettysburg.

00:48:28.940 --> 00:48:30.500
Could you comment on that?

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:33.290
ALLEN GUELZO: Robert E. Lee
began suffering symptoms

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:37.160
of angina in the 1850s when
he was then Lieutenant Colonel

00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:40.380
of the 2nd cavalry in Texas.

00:48:40.380 --> 00:48:43.250
It was referred to
then as rheumatism.

00:48:43.250 --> 00:48:45.860
But knowing the
subsequent developments

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:48.680
of his medical history,
it seems pretty plain

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:52.570
that these are the first
symptoms that we begin to see.

00:48:52.570 --> 00:48:54.680
And it shows up
more dramatically

00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:59.030
in the spring of 1863 when
he has a heart attack.

00:48:59.030 --> 00:49:00.630
He has all the classic symptoms.

00:49:00.630 --> 00:49:05.210
He's bedridden for
almost a month.

00:49:05.210 --> 00:49:10.000
And he tries to, in
letters to his wife, he--

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:12.490
because he stays with the army.

00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:16.990
He doesn't go back
to Richmond and let

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:18.610
family take care of him.

00:49:18.610 --> 00:49:20.740
He stays with his army.

00:49:20.740 --> 00:49:22.240
But the letters
that he writes, he's

00:49:22.240 --> 00:49:25.450
always trying to downplay
the seriousness of what

00:49:25.450 --> 00:49:26.390
has happened.

00:49:26.390 --> 00:49:29.770
"Well, I've got a very bad cold.

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:34.060
The doctors have been thumping
me like an old steam boiler."

00:49:34.060 --> 00:49:36.410
But it was a heart attack.

00:49:36.410 --> 00:49:39.760
He had a second one
in the fall of 1863,

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:44.980
less severe, but severe
enough that he could not ride.

00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:48.940
He had to get around in
a wagon or an ambulance.

00:49:48.940 --> 00:49:51.640
And the terms wagon and
ambulance and the Civil War

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:54.640
are almost interchangeable.

00:49:54.640 --> 00:49:59.200
He has a case of dysentery
in the spring of 1864

00:49:59.200 --> 00:50:02.530
at the apex of the
Overland Campaign.

00:50:02.530 --> 00:50:07.030
And one reason why Grant is able
to move as fast as Grant does

00:50:07.030 --> 00:50:10.900
and move around Lee is
that Lee is just not

00:50:10.900 --> 00:50:14.390
up to par in terms of health.

00:50:14.390 --> 00:50:18.580
So there are repeated health
problems that Lee deals with

00:50:18.580 --> 00:50:23.380
and which plague him and
affect his performance.

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:26.920
Bear in mind that he
is the oldest field

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:29.740
commander in the Civil War.

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:34.870
He is 56 years old at the time
of the Battle of Gettysburg.

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:41.200
Ulysses Grant, by contrast, is
41, William Tecumseh Sherman,

00:50:41.200 --> 00:50:43.990
43.

00:50:43.990 --> 00:50:46.452
You go down the list
of other major figures

00:50:46.452 --> 00:50:47.660
in the Civil War, same thing.

00:50:47.660 --> 00:50:51.350
George McClellan was 39.

00:50:51.350 --> 00:50:53.660
Lee is the oldest
and in some respects,

00:50:53.660 --> 00:50:57.530
given the rigors of 19th century
campaigning which is literally

00:50:57.530 --> 00:51:02.800
living in the rough, it takes
its toll on him physically.

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:04.390
And it is a toll
which he probably

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:05.590
should have recognized.

00:51:05.590 --> 00:51:08.920
And it leads him in August
of 1863, partly because

00:51:08.920 --> 00:51:10.990
of the defeat at
Gettysburg but also

00:51:10.990 --> 00:51:13.780
partly because of his
health considerations,

00:51:13.780 --> 00:51:17.770
to offer his resignation
to Jefferson Davis.

00:51:17.770 --> 00:51:19.900
Davis refuses.

00:51:19.900 --> 00:51:21.940
"Where," Davis says
in reply, "where

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:26.000
would I find someone
more capable of command

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:27.530
than yourself?"

00:51:27.530 --> 00:51:29.420
And that was true.

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:35.990
Because even Robert E.
Lee, plagued with angina,

00:51:35.990 --> 00:51:40.220
was infinitely more
resourceful than

00:51:40.220 --> 00:51:42.500
any other secondary
commanders who could

00:51:42.500 --> 00:51:44.870
have been put into his place.

00:51:44.870 --> 00:51:47.750
When he complained about
officers and generals not

00:51:47.750 --> 00:51:51.390
taking his orders seriously,
not carrying them out perfectly,

00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:54.320
he was not exaggerating.

00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:58.310
Who would have
taken Lee's place?

00:51:58.310 --> 00:52:02.180
There was no deep bench
in the Confederacy.

00:52:02.180 --> 00:52:04.460
So Lee soldiers on.

00:52:04.460 --> 00:52:06.260
And he goes all the
way to Appomattox.

00:52:06.260 --> 00:52:09.860
And what I think is poignant
is that he goes to Appomattox

00:52:09.860 --> 00:52:13.470
saying to William
Nelson Pendleton,

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:17.220
"I always knew that it
would turn out this way."

00:52:17.220 --> 00:52:20.230
He saw it from the beginning.

00:52:20.230 --> 00:52:22.953
AUDIENCE: Please join me
in thanking Dr. Guelzo.

00:52:22.953 --> 00:52:24.370
ALLEN GUELZO: Thank
you very much.

00:52:24.370 --> 00:52:27.720
[APPLAUSE]

